  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 253 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A cold front will continue to cross the region early \nthis morning. In the meantime, patchy dense fog, drizzle, and dreary \nskies will linger through sunrise. Conditions will then slowly \nimprove through the day as drier air flows in behind the frontal \nboundary. While not as chilly and damp as yesterday, north winds \nwill be a touch brisk with highs only getting into the mid 50s to \nlow 60s. \n\nThe remainder of the first half of the week will feature fair \nweather as high pressure settles to our north, keeping developing \nlow pressure and its associated precipitation shunted well to our \nsouth. The result will be partly to mostly sunny skies and \nseasonably cool temperatures each day. There will be a slight \nwarming trend through Thursday, with highs mainly in the 60s. Lows \nwill be in the upper 30s to low 40s each night, except in the \nsheltered valleys of the Adirondacks, Northeast Kingdom, and eastern \nVT, where temperatures will dip into the low to mid 30s. This will \nallow for patchy frost; subsequent shifts will need to monitor the \npotential for Frost Advisories. It should be noted that, as of \ntoday, these products will no longer be issued for the \nAdirondacks/Northeast Kingdom since today is the median date of the \nfirst autumn freeze. Thus, the growing season is considered ended in \nthese areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: No significant impacts are expected, but a beneficial \nrainfall is on the table for the upcoming weekend. The Friday \nthrough Sunday period remains highly uncertain for sensible weather. \nThere is potential for dry weather to continue, but also a chance \nfor widespread rain to occur, associated with an expected deep low \npressure system that tracks northward over the western Atlantic \nOcean. The 48 hour probability of precipitation from the blend of \nmodels suggests some rain is likely across south central Vermont and \nthe Upper Valley, which has trended upward compared to 12 hours ago \nbut similar to 24 hours ago. Otherwise, a coin flip chance across \nthe rest of northern Vermont and northeastern New York as far north \nas near Plattsburgh continues, and a low (under 25%) chance of any \nrain is indicated to the northwest towards the International Border \nand St. Lawrence Valley. \n\nThere have been many runs of deterministic guidance from both the \nGFS and ECMWF showing deformation bands of rain translating \nnorthward across much of Vermont, pinwheeling westward and then \ncontracting back towards the south as surface low pressure stalls \nout/retrogrades and weakens in the Saturday-Saturday night \ntimeframe. This idea is reflected in the six hour PoP trend, with \nmost widespread low chances of rain centered in the Saturday morning \nperiod. Moderate rainfall amounts are possible given this type of \nsystem, especially in our southern and eastern areas. The 90th \npercentile (reasonable high end) shows near 1\" totals possible in \ncentral Vermont increasing to near 2\" in our far southern areas. \nAgain, this type of rain would not pose any hazards but would \nobviously impact weekend outdoor activities. \n\nAdditionally, the higher terrain, especially in the central/southern \nGreens, may see unseasonably strong winds with 40 to 45 MPH easterly \nwinds at the 850 millibar level indicated by the mean of the \nensemble guidance that shows the wetter/more northward low track \namongst the possible scenarios. So potentially hazardous \nrecreational conditions, when combined with rain and temperatures in \nthe 40s, will be present this weekend. Otherwise, aside from cooler \nconditions associated with any rain, consistent temperatures near \nseasonable values should continue into the weekend.",
